A||||44||SteveJ||Worst accident in your garage?||||1||
Z||000000||SteveJ||06-22-2001||07:27 PM||||So in another thread <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011475.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011475.html</A> <BR>the young man is about to undertake the headlight repair we have all gone through. My first thought is, "Watch out, don't get your fingers in that mechanism."<P>I come to this instinctive reaction based upon my prior experience with such parts.<P>I was replacing the windshield wiper motor a couple of years ago. I can't remember how but the motor started to turn. It has a lever about 1 1/2" long on the motor shaft which swept up my right middle finger and carried it around where it jammed on the case cover.<P>I was securely held.<P>The house was too far to yell for help, but I tried.<P>The motor would not release me. Would not back up.<P>The lever had crushed into my middle finger tip. <P>What the heck to do??? I can't get loose. Thirty years of working on cars you would think I would be more careful.<P>Hurts like He_L at first but then goes numb. No useful tools in range.<P>Motor finally slid through the flesh of the finger.<P>Lots of stitches. Works fine now.<P><BR>So I read recently about another forum member who dropped an engine on his dad's front fender. <BR>Got any good stories?||||reg||1
Z||000001||ray b||06-22-2001||07:45 PM||||my dad hit a diamond ring on a grinding wheel<BR>looked for it for weeks but never found it not hurt but a large loss.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steve Normington||06-22-2001||07:45 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007769.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007769.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||06-22-2001||09:11 PM||||SteveJ--What works fine now?  Your finger or the headlight motor?<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000004||AkursedX||06-22-2001||09:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SteveJ:<BR><B><BR>So I read recently about another forum member who dropped an engine on his dad's front fender. <BR>Got any good stories?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hehehe, well I was going to mention that story, but you already did  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000005||DecadenceR||06-22-2001||09:54 PM||||Wordt thing i ever did was slip and slice my knuckle open on a valve spring. It was cool to see what my bone looked like...||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierohoho||06-22-2001||10:37 PM||||69 Buick LaSabre, backed into dad's garage and rear wheels up on ramps. Replacing the tranny, (I was 20 years old), I took the drive shaft off, I forgot to block the wheels, the car rolled forward, I was underneath, I rolled clear, the car rolled through the garage door, the door was closed, I need not say more.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
Z||000007||Patrick's Dad||06-22-2001||10:59 PM||||Not in garage, but in parking lot of Mom's apt. complex.<P>Working on 1973 Camaro with straight 6. Radiator hoses were getting soft, so I figured that I'd change them. Went out, got the parts, and went to work.<P>Top hose came off and swapped with no problem. Couldn't have been easier. I was feeling good, and assumed I'd be out of the bay in about fifteen more minutes. I loosened the worm clamp on the lower hose at the radiator. As I pulled, I came to a realization. Antifreeze poured all over the ground.<P>I'm sorry if this isn't more exciting. I haven't replaced an engine yet, but will be, hopefully, soon. Maybe I'll have something to add.||||reg||1
Z||000008||btoth||06-22-2001||11:10 PM||||My friend and I had just finished rebuilding my V6 in November of '99.  Somehow messed up the head gaskets when putting the heads back on because when we started up the car (it started really nice!) steaming coolant shot out the tailpipes and was steaming up from around the block.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  What a mess.. coolant running down the driveway and all over us and the car.  Took out the rear spark plugs and yep.. sure enough... coolant poured right out of the holes.  What a disappointment... had to do it all over again!  Haha.. funny to think about it now.  But back then, I promissed as soon as it was running I'd sell it.  Of course, I still have it now.. but with a new engine in it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000009||fierospeeder||06-22-2001||11:53 PM||||i havn't had anything major yet, besides getting shocked by the distributor when a plug wire is cracked.<P>I do have a fear of having my hand near the belt while the car running, or the car falling on me.<P>if you want some gruesome stories go to the wood forum <A HREF="http://www.forums.woodnet.net/cgi-bin/Ultimate.cgi?action=intro&BypassCookie=true" TARGET=_blank>http://www.forums.woodnet.net/cgi-bin/Ultimate.cgi?action=intro&BypassCookie=true</A> <P>just recently one person was paralized when he fell off his black and decker work table and another recent one of the danger of a planer.   Do a search for workshop mishaps, and there are gruesome ones.  ive read some of them from a few months ago. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||poupas||06-22-2001||11:56 PM||||I dont have a garage!||||reg||1
Z||000011||Terrybogin||06-23-2001||12:46 AM||||About a year ago Cooter was grinding some spot welds under a car we had clipped when the 4" Makita grinder's wheel got wedged between two of the welds. He was on a creeper, on his back and the grinder was instantly knocked from his hands and the body of the grinder hit him in the side of the head. The grinding wheel hit just above his lip and ran down his face all the way to his chest. There was a strip about 1/4 inch wide where the "meat" was just gone from his chin. He crawled out from under the car and did not think he was hurt until he saw the nice puddle in the floor. 4 hours at the E/R and a few stitches and he is good as new. For some reason, he just won't pick up the hand grinder anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000012||JSocha||06-23-2001||01:00 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://a828.g.akamai.net/7/828/1392/1dcf6501c8f9fb/images.ucomics.com/comics/ch/1986/ch860320.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Jimmy||06-23-2001||05:33 AM||||My brother and I started my Fiero on fire.  He was torching the passenger frontside cradle bolt off because it was all rusted up.  Of course the fuel lines were disconected and were dangling just above where he was working.  Next thing I saw were 1 to 2 foot high flames jumping out the top of my engine bay.  Lucky for us a friend was quick with the garden hose.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Not much got dameged.  Just  a ground wire and some wire loom melted.  <P>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000014||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-23-2001||11:06 AM||||hmm.. was'nt my garage, but a friends.. <P>worse would he decided he would squeal his tires on his Z24 as he was pulling in over the pit.. Car jumped sideways over the bar and ended up on its  side in the pit..  <P>What a job getting that car out of there!!!!<P><BR>He had just got it painted about 3 weeks before that.<P>Moral of the story?  DRIVE SLOW WHEN PULLING IN OVER A PIT hehe<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||JohnnyK||06-23-2001||12:09 PM||||Was in a bad mood and accidently drove the car through the garage door (not all the way, but enough to need a new one). It was a huge wooden 2 car garage door too...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||MrPBody||06-23-2001||12:38 PM||||In the early 1970's, working in a Buick dealership, while heating an Opel exhaust with a torch, I melted the plastic fuel line.  Fortunately I had recently attended SCCA Crash and Burn school, so I knew how to use a fire extinguisher.<P>Around 1976, I was trying to wiggle a Jag cylinder head off the head studs; the engine was sitting upright on its flywheel, on the floor.  The engine began to topple sideways, and I reached out to catch it.  (About 500 lbs. -- <I>what</I> was I thinking!)  My forearm struck my knee, with the Jag engine on top of it.  So I broke my arm over my own knee.  Just a crack, really, but it required an ER visit and a few weeks in a cast.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||c m west||06-23-2001||01:38 PM||||well, I once had my M422 ( lil' army jeep ) fall on my right arm when I was under it<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000018||Seanh||06-23-2001||03:28 PM||||Well, this really wasn't a physical accident, more stupidity on my part. Me and a friend had just finished doing the head gaskets on my 86 2.8 engine. I was putting the intake back on, and only had the mid-manifold on at the time. He was changing the spark plugs because I bought some new ones, and wanted them changed while it was easy to get to. Took 4 out without any problems, then on the 5th one, he forgot to put the little rubber boot in the plug socket and broke the plug. No big deal, it was the old set of plugs comming out anyway. Well, upon removeing the rest of the plug, he was moving it over the engine bay, and it was our luck that one little peice fell thru his hands, right into the intake port! Well, it just happened to fall right into the port with the valve WIDE OPEN and made its final resting place onto of the piston. 2 sets of head gaskets in one weekend, now thats some work!||||reg||1
Z||000019||rogergarrison||06-23-2001||07:03 PM||||did the garage door thing once. Another time i was almost done with a new all over paint job. mixed up the last coat, didnt get the cup on tite, it fell off, hit the floor and splashed the whole qt cup full in the air all over the car.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Pontiaddict||06-24-2001||04:41 AM||||My dad, my neighbor and I were installing a rebuilt 4.3 in my dads S-10. It's a 4x4 model with 5" of suspension lift, it was also on jack stands when we were bolting it back up to the transmission. Well since you couldn't really get to the engine bay with it up that high, we were standing on stuff. I was on a set of ramps with a board spanning them, my neighbor was on a step stool, while my dad was underneath the truck. The neighbor slips off the stool and ends up breaking 3 ribs on the fender.<P>I suffered severe mental anguish having to put that [use as many swear words as you want here, It still won't describe how much I hate that thing] truck back together (I really did most of the work, you know). <BR>When we <B>finally</B> got it together, we took it for a shakedown run only to have the thing overheat 10 miles from home. It seems that the petcock on the radiator was tightened down enough to hold the water in unpressurised but not enough to keep it in when it got hot and started building pressure.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Steve Normington||06-24-2001||08:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MrPBody:<BR><B>So I broke my arm over my own knee.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Do you mind if I laugh at that? BTW, did you break your own arm because you owed yourself money?<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>Around 1976, I was trying to wiggle a Jag cylinder head off the head studs; </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What kind of Jag? My dad has a 68 E-type.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||MrPBody||06-24-2001||08:23 PM||||Steve Normington --<P>You may laugh.  I did, later.<P>My Jag was a 3.8 Mk 2 sedan.  I installed a 3.8 "gold top" 3-carb engine from a Mk 9 -- much the same engine as an early 60's E-type.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000023||Wade87GT||06-25-2001||11:53 AM||||I was removing a transmission on a $100 project car ('66 Peugeot 404) when I was 17. The car was on jack stands, I was on a creeper. Had everything disconnected but something was snagging the input shaft. After yanking it, shaking it, twisting it, and doing everything else to get it off, I gave it a big shove. Instantly, it fell off and landed on my chest. Fortunately, the trans had an aluminum case (which I didn't know) and only weighed about 60 lbs. But it scared the crap out of me when it hit. Thereafter; anticipate the forces of gravity combined with thrust vectoring.||||reg||1
Z||000024||fierobaby||06-25-2001||12:11 PM||||so many garage issues...where to start...well putting the garage door down on my dads Ford F150 once was a good one good thng I wasn't the one pushin the button! :-) course then there is the thwo recent ones first smacking my head on the sunroof and the second dropping my deck lid square on my head OUCH! <P>------------------<BR>1984 Sweet Candy Apple red Fiero sport coupe :-D<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cfiero.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000025||Fierokid87||06-25-2001||12:20 PM||||News flash SteveJ, Fierobaby is not a man.  or a young man or a boy at all.  She is a Girl, a young lady, a woman,  And you  know what She is my Girlfriend who I love very<BR>much<P>Scott<P>------------------<BR>Fierokid's  Web site<BR>http://www.angelfire.com/sports/sfiero<P>AIM: Fierokid87<BR>ICQ: Fierokid<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sfiero.JPG"> ||||reg||4
Z||000026||SteveJ||06-25-2001||01:13 PM||||Ah, true love right here on the forum.<P>Wade87GT you reminded me of when I was 23. I had a Mustang with a bad transmission. I had it up on blocks and when I pulled it, it rolled off! Thank heavens I had a couple of "safety blocks" in there. The car landed on 'em but was on me too. I wasn't much thinner than the blocks. I venture to say I was as scared as you were. I had quite a time wiggling out. Hurt my shoulder too.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000027||Ken Wittlief||06-25-2001||02:43 PM||||When I was in the coast guard at base buffalo I had a '69 VW camper that had some surface rust.<P> I on weekends I was practicing using brazing rods on the arc welder (works really nice) by doing body work on my VW camper and was so proud to be doing it the right way (no bondo). I found a pack of brazing rods that worked really sweet too - had a purple flux on them - I used a whole bunch.<P> While I was working away on the side of the camper I happen to look up and notice it was full of smoke! The wood paneling on the inside had started to smolder next to where I was brazing on the outside. didnt catch but it left a nice black spot on the paneling, and a camp fire smell in the camper for months.<P>  But the funny part was at colors in the morning a week later, when all the station crews are present, and the chief engineer asks, "does anyone know what happened to the whole pack of silver solder brazing rods? They were purple..."<P>  I slowly raised my index finger and asked sheepishly, "Are they expensive?"<P>  I didnt have to pay for them, but he showed me where the regular brazing rods were and made sure I knew they were blue -NOT PURPLE!<P>  I figure that VW has about 5 lbs of silver in it, wherever it is now.||||reg||1
Z||000028||84Bill||06-25-2001||02:53 PM||||I had this posted in another thread<P>I dipped my finger in gas once and used it to light my smoke. Unfortunatly I did not know the tub water (usually for rinsing dirty hands) I was about to use to put my finger out with was full gasoline. Fortunatly the fumes went before I got my hand in there. I would not recomend smoking it's really dumb.  <BR>It was funny to watch the garage going up while the song "house of the rising sun" was playing. <BR>The fire Co arrived and quickly extinguished the blaze. Fortunatly the only damage was a melted tub and scorched benchtop and wall.<BR>||||reg||11
Z||000029||Ken Wittlief||06-25-2001||02:57 PM||||Smoking may be bad for you<P>but not as bad as smoldering, eh?||||reg||1
Z||000030||Butter||06-25-2001||04:08 PM||||Yall might like this one that happened right out side the shop in my yard.<P>  I was having starter trouble on my vw dune bug one day so I went lookin' in my shop for any old starters I had salvaged to replace it with.  All I could come up with was one 6 volt starter.  So I figured it might work for the day's ride, I had planned, if I removed the 12 volt starter nose bushing so the 6 volt would fit losely as the 6 volt starter has a bigger armatuer shaft than the 12 volt starters.  So I did this and thought I'd bump the starter button to see if this would work.  I had neglected to take the thing out of gear but I did have the coil disabled.  This was about the time my buddies were pulling up to go riding.  The starter engaged and I got right off the button as the buggy began to go forward.  As luck would have it the bendix locked engaged and the starter did not stop turning when I got off the button.  As the buggy started to move it caught my shirt on the edge of the passenger's seat and here we go across the yard together. I could not get at the shifter cause I was busy trying to stay in front of the back tire and figure how to stop this beast.  Now right before it starts out the driveway I get the battery post off and get it stopped.  This is about the time everybody's tryin' to figure out what I was doin' and what was going on.  Must have looked some awful funny as they seen me getting dragged across the yard and hear no engine running.  Even I busted out laughn' right after I got it stopped.  They was wantin' to know just what had happened.  So I thought quick and told the truth.  I said that's what happens when you put 6 where a 12 is supposed to be!<P>||||reg||7
Z||000031||Ken Wittlief||06-26-2001||09:51 AM||||after reading this thread I guess I've been lucky - the worst thing that has happened in my garage was when my son, 3 at the time, fell off a rubber maid stool (about 8" high) and broke his wrist.<P> but I guess the people that have had the bad garage accidents arnt around anymore to tell us what happened to them.<P> [Reflective pause to count my blessings]||||reg||1
Z||000032||Ken Wittlief||06-26-2001||09:57 AM||||Hey I can tell you the coolest thing I ever did in a garage! At the coast guard station in rochester we had three full sets of combination box/open end wrenches, two standard and one metric, all hanging on the wall.<P> one day I was messing around with two large ones, the 1 " and the 15/16" - you know how you can hook the open end of one into the box end of another to increase leverage?<P>  I was playing around with this, and ended up with a 1" wrench tightly clamped in the bench vise, with the 15/16" hooked into that, and the 7/8" hooked into that... all three sets all hooked together so it spanned about ten feet across the floor - about 40 wrenches in all right down to the little 1/8" ignition wrenches.<P>  it looked like some weird chrome metal art sculpture - I almost hated to take it all down 3 days later when someone needed a wrench.||||reg||1
Z||000033||FieroSTETZ||06-26-2001||03:31 PM||||friend of mine was installing a new clutch in his 440 charger 4spd, and after getting the clutch in, started it to pull it off the ramps. the clutch had some "new clutch fuzz" which caused it to get EXCELLENT grip, and the build 440 flung one of the ramps into the garage and through the wooden door between the garage and the house. that was cool, almost took out his dad on the way though<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.shawnrocks.com/Animation1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000034||Steve Normington||06-26-2001||05:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>I was playing around with this, and ended up with a 1" wrench tightly clamped in the bench vise, with the 15/16" hooked into that, and the 7/8" hooked into that... all three sets all hooked together so it spanned about ten feet across the floor - about 40 wrenches in all right down to the little 1/8" ignition wrenches.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ladies and Gentlemen, I give you the quintessential definition of too much time on your hands.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000035||Raydar||06-26-2001||09:00 PM||||This happened to a guy I knew in high school.<BR>Had a 68 Camaro SS, with a 427. He rebuilt the 427 several times, going from L88 to ZL1 specs. During one of these rebuilds, he had the block in a "bear hug", (he was half gorilla) and was carrying it across his garage. Dropped it on his foot. <BR>Still hurts to think about.<P>Worst thing that ever happened to me was when I was trying to remove a steering wheel nut from my 68 Fairlane. Had one wrench on the nut and was banging on it with another wrench, trying to break it loose.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> Brought the "hammer" wrench down squarely on my thumb. Right on the knuckle. Thought it was broken, but got lucky.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"||||reg||1
Z||000036||Wade87GT||06-26-2001||10:10 PM||||Butter's "incident" reminds me of another.<BR>There I Was, troubleshooting a fuel delivery problem on my 1980 Triumph TR-8. The circuit runs through the oil pressure switch, no oil pressure and the fuel pump doesn't get energized. So, I pull the connector from the the oil pump, disconnect the fuel line at the carb, put the end in a container, turn on the key, and use a jumper wire. Bingo, fuel pump works, but the container falls and fuel spurts everywhere. So I yank the jumper. Of course, I need to try it again to visualize consistant flow. After clean-up, I try it again. To my surprize, the starter engages and the car lurches rearward (shifter was in reverse). Since the hood is hinged at the front and I'm leaning over the engine, it snags me. Everything happens so quick and as we're almost out of the garage, I pull out the jumper wire. Ain't no tellin how we could have ended up. Turns out the wiring diagram in the Factory Repair Manual doesn't match my car and I inadvertantly jumped the starter wire. I know now that I'm supposed to use the diagram for the 12 right hand drive 1981 cars  built for the UK market. ||||reg||3
Z||000037||MrPBody||06-28-2001||09:55 AM||||Reminds me of another one . . .<P>I was tuning a Jag E-type automatic, bent over the engine with the hood (bonnet) open.  [For anyone who doesn't know, an E-type's entire nose pivots at the front for engine access.  And it's heavy!]  My friend Al sitting in the driver's seat accidentally knocks the car into reverse.  As the car rolled back, I straightened-up, Al jammed on the brakes, and the bonnet slammed shut where I had been only a second earlier.  No injury, but it was close enough for the bonnet to snatch a pen out of my shirt pocket!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000038||SteveJ||06-28-2001||11:23 AM||||OK, after the last two I'll tell about mine. I was given an '80 I think it was Granada that had carburetor problems. It even came with a second carburetor in the trunk that had yet to be installed.<BR>I put it on. Car ran pretty good but had idle problems especially in drive. I put some blocks in front of the front tires and put it in drive up against the blocks. ( You can see what's coming can't you )<BR>Twisted the idle speed and mixture screws and go it pretty good. Well to see if it would settle down after the throttle was off idle I gave the cable a little pull. <BR>Naturally the car jumped the blocks crashing into my propane grill and Troy Built rototiller. Fortunately I had been standing on the side.<BR>Can't explain why I did that. What the heck was I thinking of. I could easily have told you the result of my actions before I did it. Just too focused on what I was doing to think of other factors I guess. ||||reg||1
Z||000039||Ken Wittlief||06-28-2001||11:46 AM||||I know a guy who was working on his station wagon under the hood, and snapped something off with a rachet socket wrench (I dont remember what)<P> he slammed the hood down, and threw the socket wrench through the windshield. Im not sure where the accident part of this left off - but he junked the car after that.||||reg||1
Z||000040||Ken Wittlief||06-28-2001||11:51 AM||||I also discovered something by accident in my garage once - my car was really dirty so I washed it good with ERA laundry soap. Then I waxed it with turtle paste wax.<P>  while I was waxing the car I rinsed the sponge out a few times in the soapy ERA water bucket, then with clear water.<P>  I got distracted and didnt dump the bucket. A few hours later I discovered the whole bucket of soapy waxy water had gelled - gelled pretty good like putting three boxes of jello in a cup of water. I dont know what the goop was, but it sure was weird!||||reg||1
Z||000041||SteveJ||06-28-2001||03:28 PM||||In the army they told us that soap and gasoline makes a pretty good napalm. So you had soap and wax I guess.||||reg||1
Z||000042||artherd||06-29-2001||06:49 AM||||Oh my, where to start?<P>Well, I was one of those kids who fell off a ladder in the garage too.  Only I was two years old.  And it was an 8-foot aluminum ladder. And a concreate floor.  It explains my lack of spelling ability to this day.<P>I've had a flywheel explode at over 17,000RPM not two feet away from my face.  (totally exposed, it was on a twin cylender 2-stroke motor I was building for a project kart.)<P>Thank gods three times I wasn't in the plane of rotation, there are still pieces of it embeded into the celing... and the 2x4s...<P>I've also burned my face off.  With rocket fuel.    I was mixing up some solid rocket fuel, and of course in order to do so you need to MELT it.  I used a rig I built from a Weller regulated soldering iron.    I was wearing a full-face welding-style mask, AND eyegoggles underneeth that.  And welding gloves.  Just in case.  And I was working with only 2oz at a time (with the rest stored in their own fireproof steel canisters, 50' away)<P>Somehow, to this day I still do not know how, it ignited.  By the time I could scream, my face shield, and mask, had been blown off. <BR>And to my face I suffered essentially a bad sunburn all around (the safety gear took the brunt, and were actually probally sucked off in the resultant vaccum just after the blast, the still hot but rising fireball burned my face from a distance.)  I have finally decided that the molten 'solid' rocket fuel was lit off by some piece of airborne dust that managed to sneak it's way into the fuel, and reached it's flashpoint.<P>The moral? work on rocket fuel in a nitrogen filled clean room. by remote. With robots.<P>PS: I came out better for the whole deal, never had any problems with acne EVER again after that!<P>And I won't even TELL you about the times I rigged up some carbon rods and 8 led acid car batteries and an arc welder and an oxyaccetalene torch to produce a plasma arc torch (around 20-30,000F) Suffice it to say you'd be amazed what burns under that kind of ungodly heat.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000043||Wade87GT||06-29-2001||02:41 PM||||Whoa artherd, you planning to go back to the future? Tell us you didn't build that car with the rocket motors they found imbedded on the side of a mountain. I do like the quote in your signature though and sounds like you're living up to it.||||reg||3
